# BeamOnTarget simple example beam configuration
#
# 50 randomly placed beamlets travelling along +x, aimed at the cube and sphere
# in GEOMETRY_EXAMPLE/. Generated with numpy default_rng(seed=20260918), so the
# file is reproducible; regenerate it only if you also update this header.
#
#   beamlets      50, random positions on the source plane x = -3.0 m
#   particles     H- (mass 1.67e-27 kg, charge -1)
#   energy        100 keV, monoenergetic
#   direction     +x, with ~2 mrad Gaussian jitter in y and z
#   divergence    5 mrad core, 30 mrad halo, halo fraction 0.1
#   beamlet size  sigma_y = sigma_z = 1 mm
#   current dens. 31.830988618379067 A/m^2 (= 100/pi), chosen with BEAMLET_RADIUS_M = 0.01
#                 to give exactly 1 kW per beamlet, 50 kW total
#   spread        y in [-1.25, 1.60] m, z in [-0.50, 0.50] m
#
# Roughly a third of the beamlets strike the cube, a third the sphere, and a
# third pass between them -- so the summary reports intercepted power that is
# less than the total incident power, which is the normal situation.
#
# Columns are whitespace separated; lines beginning with '#' are ignored.
